.TH BLOCKWRIGHT-CLI 1 2026-11-19 "Blockwright 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blockwright-cli \- convert, render, export and check ANSI and IRC block art
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blockwright-cli
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blockwright-cli
is Blockwright without the window: it reads ANSI (.ans), XBIN (.xb), BIN
(.bin) and Blockwright projects (a .bwp zip or a project directory, read as
its flattened composite), and converts, renders, exports, shades and lints
them.
.B blockwright-cli \-\-help
lists every option and which commands take it.
.PP
An .ans is read as UTF-8 when it starts with a byte order mark, or when every
byte from 0x80 up belongs to well-formed UTF-8 and at least one character
takes three or four bytes; otherwise it is read as CP437.
.BR "\-\-encoding auto" ", " cp437 " or " utf\-8
sets it for
.BR info ", " convert ", " render ", " remap ", " export ", " shade ", " lint ", " pack
and
.BR unpack .
SAUCE fields are CP437 either way.
.BR convert ", " shade ", " remap
and
.B replay
write an .ans as CP437, or as UTF-8 with
.BR "\-\-output\-encoding utf\-8"
(SAUCE stays CP437, and a byte order mark is written only when the text has
no character of three bytes or more); an XBIN or BIN
.I out
is CP437, so utf\-8 refuses one.
.B snapshot
writes the project's own encoding, or the one
.B \-\-output\-encoding
gives.
Everything else is written as CP437.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI info " file"
Dimensions, SAUCE fields and, for an .ans, the text encoding it was read as;
for a project, the encoding the app writes its next .ans in.
.TP
.BI convert " in out"
Write an .ans back out as .ans, XBIN or BIN, by the name of
.IR out ;
a .bwp
.I out
is refused, since
.B pack
writes a project;
.B \-\-output\-encoding
picks the .ans text encoding;
.BI \-\-palette " P"
gives the piece palette
.IR P ,
a preset (cga, cga\-yellow, xterm, windows, campbell, mirc) or a palette file
(an XBIN, a GIMP .gpl, a JASC .pal or a hex colour a line), without changing a
cell. Only XBIN and a project hold a palette: an .ans or BIN
.I out
of a piece with its own palette is lossy.
.TP
.BI remap " in out"
With
.BI \-\-map " M"
(FROM=TO pairs such as 3=11,11=3, or 16 hex digits, digit n what n becomes),
map the cells' colour indices; with
.BI \-\-fit\-palette " P"
give the piece palette
.I P
and map each index to the entry of
.I P
nearest its colour, counting the cells that change colour. The palette of a
plain remap stays. Written as
.B convert
writes.
.TP
.BI render " in out.png"
Draw a piece as a PNG with the IBM VGA font, in its own palette or the one
.B \-\-palette
gives;
.B \-\-irc
draws the IRC view, each line cut at
.B \-\-budget
bytes (1 to 512; default: a project's own, else 407), with
.BR \-\-fit ", " \-\-pad " and " \-\-swap\-shades
applied as in
.BR "export \-\-irc" ;
a .bwp
.I out
is refused.
.TP
.BI "export \-\-irc" " in out"
Write IRC text with mIRC colour codes and report each line's size in bytes;
a .bwp
.I out
is refused.
.TP
.BI "export \-\-micron" " in out"
Write a Micron page (.mu) for NomadNet: the piece in its own colours,
left-aligned, or centred on the page with
.BR \-\-centre ;
.BI \-\-cache " S"
writes the page header
.BI #!c= S\fR,\fP
the seconds a peer may cache the page (0: never).
A cell Micron can't write is written as
.B ?
with a warning; a .bwp
.I out
is refused.
.TP
.BI shade " in out"
Lighten or darken cells along a ramp's shading ladder, or run the form,
despeckle or rim-light brush over a box or a mask; a .bwp
.I out
is refused, and
.B \-\-output\-encoding
picks the .ans text encoding.
.TP
.BI lint " in"
Check the shading ladders, pixel noise and the craft rules; exits 4 when
there are findings.
A note (ans\-loss, under
.BR "\-\-target irc" )
is printed with
.B note:
before its message and doesn't count.
.TP
.BI "library list" " \fR[\fPdir\fR]\fP"
List the stamps and text styles in a library.
.TP
.BI "library check" " dir"
Check every stamp, sidecar and text style in a library.
.TP
.BI pack " in out.bwp"
Write a piece or a project as a project zip.
.TP
.BI unpack " in dir"
Write a piece or a project as a project directory.
.TP
.BI snapshot " project"
Save the flattened composite as the project's next round, as an .ans in the
project's encoding, or in the one
.B \-\-output\-encoding
gives.
.TP
.BI add-generator " project dir"
Add a generator layer from a directory with a generator.json, run it once and
save the project.
.TP
.BI run-generators " project"
Run the project's visible generator layers again and save their output.
A hidden generator layer is not run, and is listed as
.RI "layer " id " (" name "): hidden, not run;"
.BI \-\-layer " id"
runs only the layers it names, hidden ones included.
.TP
.BI import-scene " scene.py out"
Open a Python scene as a new project with a generator layer for each of its
layers, iCE on unless the scene's layer list says
.BR "\(dqice\(dq: false" .
To rebuild a project byte for byte,
.B \-\-bake
writes each layer as the cells it made, without its script, so the project
doesn't name the scene's path;
.BI \-\-author " name"
(1 to 20 CP437 characters) and
.BI \-\-date " CCYYMMDD"
set the SAUCE author and date, and make a SAUCE record when the scene's title
gave none.
.TP
.BI replay " steps.json " \-o " dir"
Apply a steps file's edits to its start (a file, a bundled sample or a blank
canvas) through the editor's brushes, shapes, selection and layer operations,
and write the flattened canvas after each step as
.IR NN \- name .ans,
or
.IR NN \- name \-before.ans
and
.IR NN \- name \-after.ans
for a step marked for both;
.B \-\-project
and
.B \-\-render
write a .bwp and a PNG beside each. Nothing is written when a step can't be
applied: the error names the step. Every file is written under a temporary
name and renamed into place once all are written, so a write that fails
leaves no new file; files already in
.I dir
are left unless the run writes one of the same name.
A capture whose start has no SAUCE record gets one dated today, or the day of
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
when it is set (see ENVIRONMENT).
.B "blockwright-cli replay \-\-help"
gives the steps file's format.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the commands and options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the commit it was built from, and exit.
.PP
The other options belong to particular commands; see
.BR "blockwright-cli \-\-help" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. When it is set and not empty,
.B import-scene
dates the project's SAUCE record with that day in UTC instead of today, unless
.B \-\-date
is given, and
.B replay
dates the SAUCE record it gives a capture whose start has none with that day
instead of today. A value that isn't digits, or is past the end of 9999, is a usage
error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An input that can't be read or an output that can't be written.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.TP
.B 3
.B export \-\-irc
found a line over the byte budget.
.TP
.B 4
.B lint
found something other than a note.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR blockwright (1)
